<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

return [
    'stack_heading' => 'The whole stack in questions & answers',
    'stack_p1' => 'How MECM, database, portal, PowerShell, Ansible, ESXi and the machine API work together: from the web interface to the finished Windows VM. Click a question to expand it.',
    'stack_q14' => 'How do forms present hints and input errors?',
    'stack_a14_p1' => 'A hint is technically associated with exactly the field or field group it explains. A screen reader therefore announces it together with the control name. Hints that only apply to a particular deployment mode become part of the description only in that state.',
    'stack_a14_p2' => 'After an unsuccessful save, the message stays next to the affected field. The field is announced as invalid and also references its error text, so colour is never the only indicator.',
    'stack_q1' => 'What are the parts of the system?',
    'stack_a1_p1' => 'VirtuSphere is not a single program but an interplay of several building blocks. Each one has a clearly bounded job, and none knows the internals of the others; they only exchange data over a few fixed interfaces:',
    'stack_a1_li1' => 'The portal: this web interface, a PHP application. Planning, monitoring and administration happen here; heavy, long-running work is deliberately not executed by it.',
    'stack_a1_li2' => 'The MySQL database: the memory of the system. Missions, VMs, network interfaces, jobs, credentials and logs live here.',
    'stack_a1_li3' => 'The deploy worker: a background service that processes the job queue and triggers the Ansible runs.',
    'stack_a1_li4' => 'The maintenance service: a second background service for cleanup jobs, ESXi inventory scheduling and its own heartbeat.',
    'stack_a1_li5' => 'Ansible on a Linux host reachable via SSH: executes the actual ESXi commands (create, power, read out VMs).',
    'stack_a1_li6' => 'The ESXi server: the virtualisation platform where the VMs actually come to life.',
    'stack_a1_li7' => 'The MECM server with four scheduled tasks, three for synchronisation and one for the MECM site state, plus the Windows clients themselves: they install Windows and report progress back.',
    'stack_a1_p2' => 'Portal, database and the two background services run together as Docker containers on an Ubuntu host; the next question explains this runtime environment in more detail.',
    'stack_q2' => 'What does the portal itself run on? (Ubuntu and Docker)',
    'stack_a2_p1' => 'The entire portal runs on a single Ubuntu server on the LAN, fully inside Docker containers. Docker wraps each service in its own isolated environment with a pinned version; apart from Docker itself, nothing needs to be installed on the Ubuntu host. Everything is started and managed together via Docker Compose (the "docker compose" command in the project directory). These containers belong to the stack:',
    'stack_a2_li1' => 'webserver: nginx, accepts all HTTP requests from the LAN (port 8021) and hands PHP pages to the PHP container.',
    'stack_a2_li2' => 'php: runs the PHP code of the portal and the machine API (PHP 8.4).',
    'stack_a2_li3' => 'mysql: the database (MySQL 8.4); its data lives permanently in a data directory on the host and survives every container restart.',
    'stack_a2_li4' => 'deploy-worker and maintenance-worker: the two background services for deployments and maintenance.',
    'stack_a2_li5' => 'phpmyadmin: a database administration interface for administrators. It does not start with the stack; it is started on demand through the Docker Compose "tools" profile and is only reachable from the server itself.',
    'stack_a2_p2' => 'The containers carry the "unless-stopped" restart rule: after a crash or Ubuntu-host reboot, containers return unless they were deliberately stopped before. A supervisor deliberately remains manual when an old child is unresolved or its restart budget cannot be read. The system is built for LAN-only operation: no internet access required, no external services and no product telemetry. Communication starts over HTTP; switching to HTTPS is a later administrative step.',
    'stack_q3' => 'Where is which data stored, and what is the "truth"?',
    'stack_a3_p1' => 'The portal\'s MySQL database is the memory for the desired state: missions, VMs, network interfaces, jobs, credentials and logs. Credentials (ESXi, Ansible) are never stored in plaintext but encrypted in the database. "Desired state" means: the portal describes what the VMs are supposed to look like. On ESXi a change only takes effect reliably once the VM is created there anew.',
    'stack_a3_p2' => 'MECM is the truth for the end of a device\'s life: deletion there is deliberately manual only, so that no automatism accidentally removes productive devices. Package definitions live as config.json folders on the MECM server, operating systems as Task Sequences; both catalogs are only displayed in the portal, not created or edited there.',
    'stack_a3_p3' => 'For the virtualization layer, ESXi itself is the truth: datacenters, datastores and port groups are maintained there. The portal reads these values read-only at fixed intervals and keeps only a mirror of them, the ESXi inventory. The mirror warns about ordinary deviations but does not block them. Two separate gates remain fail-closed: deriving an empty mission datacenter needs exactly one confirmed exact name inside the fixed evidence window, and a freshly proven free ESXi licence has no write API. Changes are always made on the ESXi host, never in the portal.',
    'stack_a3_p4' => 'This split is intentional: every piece of information has exactly one place where it is changed. To create or change a VM, use the portal; to change a package, the MECM server; to dispose of a device, the MECM console; to change a datastore or port group, the ESXi host. Every other place only shows a mirror of it, which is exactly why they cannot be maintained contradictorily.',
    'stack_q4' => 'What happens when I queue a deploy job?',
    'stack_a4_p1' => 'The portal only writes the job into the database queue; it does not execute anything itself. There is a practical reason: an Ansible run can take many minutes, and a web page should not block that long. The actual work is done by the deploy worker, a dedicated container that continuously watches the queue:',
    'stack_a4_li1' => 'The deploy worker picks up the next waiting job from the queue.',
    'stack_a4_li2' => 'It generates the Ansible files (inventory and variables) with all VM data of the mission.',
    'stack_a4_li3' => 'It uploads the files via SFTP to the Ansible host and starts the playbook there via SSH.',
    'stack_a4_li4' => 'Ansible talks to the ESXi server and creates the VMs, powers them on or off or reads them out, depending on the chosen mode.',
    'stack_a4_li5' => 'The run output streams back live into the deploy log; at the end the job is marked as succeeded or failed.',
    'stack_a4_p2' => 'Further jobs simply wait in the queue meanwhile. Processing follows the order of queueing, with two exceptions: a job with a schedule is only picked up once its time has come, and mission deploys take their turn before the mission-less system jobs of the ESXi inventory fetches. Waiting fetches therefore never overtake a deploy; only a fetch that is already running still finishes first, because the worker executes exactly one job at a time. If the deploy worker is down, nothing is lost: the jobs stay in the queue and continue after its restart.',
    'stack_q5' => 'How do the VMs\' MAC addresses get into the portal?',
    'stack_a5_p1' => 'ESXi only assigns MAC addresses once the VM has been created. Before that, the VM exists in the portal only as a planning entry without a MAC, which is exactly why MECM cannot know it at that point.',
    'stack_a5_p2' => 'In the power-cycle step the VMs are briefly powered on and off again; the playbook then reads the MACs from ESXi and sends them via HTTP to the portal\'s machine API. Under the current job, attempt and execution fence, the portal accepts only the interface whose portgroup exactly matches the mission\'s "WDS portgroup for PXE". Empty, exactly duplicated, case-mismatched or ambiguous targets and a MAC already bound to another VM are rejected without changing VM state.',
    'stack_a5_p3' => 'Only with a known MAC can MECM reliably recognise the VM later, because during PXE boot the MAC is the only attribute the device can identify itself with. If the upload fails (wrong API base URL, missing IP allowlist entry), the VMs get stuck without a MAC and never reach the MECM handover; this is the most common stumbling block when setting up a new environment.',
    'stack_q6' => 'How does MECM learn about new VMs?',
    'stack_a6_p1' => 'The MECM server fetches them itself: the "device sync" PowerShell script (scheduled task) asks the portal API at short intervals for VMs with a MAC address. The interval is configured on the MECM server during installation; the value actually in effect is shown on System status in that source\'s row under "Interval". The row names the cadence the task really runs on: a value written by hand outside the range allowed for that task is corrected at start, and the task writes the correction to its daily log. So where the row differs from what was configured, the reason is there. The portal never accesses MECM itself; the scripts always come to it. This pull principle simplifies firewall and permissions: the MECM server only needs HTTP or HTTPS access to the portal, nothing else.',
    'stack_a6_p2' => 'The script imports new VMs as devices into MECM, approves them and assigns them via direct membership to their collections: the collection of their operating system (folder VirtuSphere_OS), the collections of their assigned packages and the collection of their mission (folder VirtuSphere_Missions). Missing collections and folders are created by the script itself. What is imported is the VM\'s Windows hostname, not its ESXi name: in MECM the device is therefore called something like "Backup-12345", not "VM-12345".',
    'stack_a6_p3' => 'Which MECM device belongs to a VM is decided in a fixed order. If a ResourceID is already bound, only that one counts, and a differing display name in MECM is explicitly not an error: Windows may rename itself later, and MECM picks that up through Discovery. Without a binding, an existing device is only adopted when name and MAC both point uniquely at it. Everything else, meaning a name with a foreign MAC, a MAC with a foreign name, several matches or a predecessor device that has not been deleted yet, is a conflict: the script changes nothing, does not report the ResourceID back, and the VM stays in the queue until a person decides. Only the reported ResourceID moves the VM to stage 4/5 in the portal.',
    'stack_a6_p4' => 'The name travels a fixed chain, and every station has exactly one owner: the desired value in the portal (field "Windows hostname") is frozen as the rollout name at the first hand-off to MECM; MECM imports that frozen name and passes it to the client; the client renames Windows to it; and from then on Windows itself may update the display name in MECM through Discovery. A later correction of the desired value in the portal therefore changes neither the running Windows machine nor the already bound MECM record. It applies to the next rollout only, and the one thing that activates it is "Reset MECM ID".',
    'stack_q7' => 'How does Windows get onto the VM?',
    'stack_a7_p1' => 'Via PXE network boot: the VM boots from its network interface in the WDS VLAN, the only VLAN where a PXE server answers. MECM recognises the device by its MAC and starts the task sequence of its operating system collection, which installs Windows fully automatically; which task sequence that is, is determined solely by the operating system chosen for the VM in the portal.',
    'stack_a7_p2' => 'After the Windows base installation the assigned packages take over: in MECM they are bound to their package collections as required deployments, and since the device sync has put the VM into exactly these collections, Software Center installs them without further intervention.',
    'stack_a7_p3' => 'In parallel the four client phases run (getinfo, hostname, staticip, disks): small scripts that in order write device information, conditionally rename the computer, change the Windows IP configuration and activate additional disks. Their reports to the "Client phases" section of the "Edit VM" page are best effort and can be incomplete because of their control path or a delivery failure. The revision-bound client-ready acknowledgement at the end of getinfo is separate: the portal moves the VM to stage 5/5 as soon as it accepts the acknowledgement. The client sets positive local detection only after the acknowledgement response and retries otherwise. Neither the stage nor phase telemetry alone therefore proves that rename, network and disks are complete.',
    'stack_q8' => 'Where does the package list in the portal come from?',
    'stack_a8_p1' => 'From MECM, fully automatically in two steps. First: the autoimporter on the MECM server watches a package folder there. To provide a new package, drop a folder with the installation files and a small config.json (name, version, options); the script autonomously builds the MECM application including collection and deployment in the console folder VirtuSphere_Applications.',
    'stack_a8_p2' => 'Second: the package sync regularly reports the contents of exactly this folder via HTTP to the portal. The name is split at the last hyphen into base name and version ("Firefox-115.0" becomes Firefox in version 115.0; the base name may itself contain hyphens, the version may not). If a package disappears from MECM it is not deleted in the portal but marked as "Retired"; a configurable protection threshold additionally prevents a faulty run from accidentally retiring large parts of the catalog at once.',
    'stack_a8_p3' => 'The portal only displays the catalog and links packages to VMs; creating, changing or deleting packages happens exclusively on the MECM server. The same holds for operating systems: they come as Task Sequences via the same sync and are only displayed in the portal, not maintained. That way there is exactly one place of maintenance and no two competing catalogs that could drift apart.',
    'stack_q9' => 'Who connects to whom, and in which direction?',
    'stack_a9_p1' => 'Almost everything comes to the portal, not the other way round. The connections at a glance:',
    'stack_a9_li1' => 'Browser to portal (HTTP/HTTPS): humans operate the portal.',
    'stack_a9_li2' => 'MECM scripts to portal (HTTP/HTTPS): fetch VM lists, report device IDs, package catalog and run results.',
    'stack_a9_li3' => 'Windows clients to portal (HTTP/HTTPS): report client phases. These are PowerShell scripts as well; they are distributed to the freshly installed VMs via the MECM Software Center and run locally there.',
    'stack_a9_li4' => 'Ansible host to portal (HTTP/HTTPS): upload the MAC addresses read from ESXi.',
    'stack_a9_li5' => 'Portal to Ansible host (SSH/SFTP): upload deployment files and start playbooks.',
    'stack_a9_li6' => 'Portal to MECM server: not required. The portal never connects out to the MECM server; result reports and the site state come from the MECM server to the portal.',
    'stack_a9_li7' => 'Ansible host to ESXi (API): create, power and read out VMs.',
    'stack_a9_p2' => 'These directions are deliberate: no external system needs credentials to the portal, the firewall only has to allow a few paths, and a compromised client could at most fake its own reports. Incoming machine access is additionally protected by IP allowlisting and an optional token.',
    'stack_q10' => 'What does "API" refer to?',
    'stack_a10_p1' => 'Besides the portal pages for humans, the portal has a machine interface: individual HTTP endpoints through which the MECM scripts fetch VM lists and report device IDs and package catalogs, Ansible delivers the MAC addresses, and the Windows clients report their installation phases.',
    'stack_a10_p2' => 'It is protected in several layers: it is only reachable on the LAN; server access (MECM, Ansible) must be on the IP allowlist (portal, "Settings" page); the server heartbeats can additionally require a shared token; and Windows clients identify themselves via a MAC address the portal already knows, so they need no entry of their own and no token.',
    'stack_a10_p3' => 'There is no web interface for the machine API; what happens there shows up indirectly in System status and Logs (important events). Nobody needs to know the exact endpoints and data formats for daily operations; they do not change without a deliberate migration decision, because the scripts are built firmly on top of them.',
    'stack_q13' => 'How does the portal know whether MECM and its tasks are running?',
    'stack_a13_p1' => 'The portal never queries MECM itself; it waits for best-effort reports. The three synchronisation tasks attempt to report start and completion with success, warning or failure for each run; the site-health task reports completion only. A missing report does not prove what happened in MECM. System status therefore shows the last report that arrived and its time, separately from whether the source has since become overdue.',
    'stack_a13_p2' => 'A fourth task additionally reports the official state of the MECM site itself, in green, yellow or red, exactly as MECM shows it in the console under Monitoring. That is why the portal shows two separate signals: the data flow (Integration) and the MECM site state. Read them separately, because the data flow can run cleanly while MECM itself reports a warning, and the other way round.',
    'stack_q11' => 'What happens when a component fails?',
    'stack_a11_p1' => 'The system has bounded recovery paths. It repeats automatically only what is safe under current identity and evidence; unclear external effects stop for manual resolution. Typical cases:',
    'stack_a11_li1' => 'MECM server or one of its scripts fails: new VMs still run through deployment up to the MAC callback and then stay at stage 3/5; the source in System status reports "Down". After restart, the sync continues ordinary pending entries and recreates required managed console folders when needed. Uncertain membership journals, old content tracking state and unproven external effects deliberately block and name the required manual check instead.',
    'stack_a11_li2' => 'Portal briefly unreachable: the channels behave differently. The revision-bound client-ready acknowledgement remains retryable through missing local detection and server-side idempotency. Client phase reports have a single best-effort delivery attempt and can remain missing; their local log and MECM detection are then authoritative. MECM tasks run again later, but have no outbox that recreates an individual lost result report.',
    'stack_a11_li3' => 'Deploy worker down: jobs that were not claimed remain in the queue. A job that already started and has an unclear external effect gets no blind replacement run; its recovery state and log may require a manual decision.',
    'stack_a11_li4' => 'Ansible host unreachable: depending on already proven external effects, a job ends as failed, partially successful or unresolved and writes its bounded diagnostics to the deployment log. Review the result, VM units, identity and recovery guidance first; queue only a permitted retry. The ESXi inventory pull also runs through this host and then reports the "Ansible host" category.',
    'stack_a11_li5' => 'ESXi unreachable or login wrong: the inventory fetch fails and the traffic light changes colour, but the last known data is kept; nothing is emptied. On a login failure the automatic fetch pauses until the credential is changed, so the ESXi account does not get locked.',
    'stack_a11_li6' => 'Ubuntu host reboots: containers with the Compose "unless-stopped" rule return unless they were deliberately stopped before. The scheduled tasks on the MECM server also have a system-start trigger. A supervisor with an unresolved old child or unreadable restart budget deliberately remains manual and starts no replacement.',
    'stack_a11_p2' => 'Backups replace lost data. They do not replace an ownership decision about a possibly executed external step, a damaged journal, or a site prerequisite such as credentials, allowlists, provider access, distribution points or networking. Resolve those cases from System status and logs before repeating or releasing anything.',
    'stack_q12' => 'How is the system backed up, and how do you restore?',
    'stack_a12_p1' => 'On the Ubuntu host, "sh scripts/backup.sh" writes a compressed database dump, a configuration archive (.env, Compose and nginx configuration), and the SHA-256 manifest for both archives to Docker/backups/ per run. The script checks the dump and retains the newest files of each artifact type (14 by default); a restore requires all three files with the same timestamp. A daily cron job plus a copy to a second host is recommended, because the backups contain secrets and belong in an access-restricted place.',
    'stack_a12_p2' => 'Whether a backup works for the checked state is established only by the restore drill: "sh scripts/restore_test.sh" selects the manifest and both archives from the same requested data root, checks hashes and archive paths, loads the dump into a throwaway MySQL, runs migrations, and checks schema, invariants, credential decryption plus health, login and machine-API smokes without touching the running stack. This does not replace a real disaster restore on the target host. The package definitions (config.json folders) live on the MECM server and must be included in its own backup there. The complete guide is in the project directory on the server (not reachable inside the portal): docs/operations/backup.md.',
    'ops_heading' => 'Day-to-day operations',
    'ops_p1' => 'A short routine for rotating admin staff. Detailed steps are in the operations docs (docs/operations).',
    'ops_daily' => 'Daily: keep an eye on the dashboard banner and System status.',
    'ops_weekly' => 'Weekly: spot-check the log viewer, review the backup card on the settings page.',
    'ops_monthly' => 'Monthly: run the restore proof (restore_test.sh).',
    'ops_jobs_heading' => 'Where and when the background jobs run',
    'ops_jobs_p1' => 'There is exactly one cron job, and it lives on the Docker host, not in the portal and not in a container. Everything else is a long-running loop inside the stack that needs no schedule:',
    'ops_jobs_backup' => 'Backup (the only cron job): an entry in /etc/cron.d/virtusphere-backup on the Docker host, usually daily at 06:00. It starts scripts/backup.sh, which writes the MySQL dump and a configuration archive to Docker/backups/. The script checks the dump for minimum size and gzip integrity and prunes older runs. Its output goes to the log /var/log/virtusphere-backup.log.',
    'ops_jobs_deploy' => 'Deploy worker: the deploy-worker container, no cron. It asks for due deploy jobs every :seconds seconds and runs them in order. Scheduled jobs wait until their time has come.',
    'ops_jobs_maintenance' => 'Maintenance worker: the maintenance-worker container, no cron. It wakes every :sleep seconds and runs jobs on their own intervals: purging of old logs, heartbeat evaluation, ESXi inventory scheduling.',
    'ops_jobs_esxi' => 'ESXi inventory fetch: also the maintenance worker, on the interval configured under Settings (default every :hours hours).',
    'ops_jobs_p2' => 'One command on the host installs or changes the backup schedule; editing the crontab by hand is not needed:',
    'ops_jobs_p3' => 'On every run backup.sh reads that cron file back and reports the schedule it found to the portal. The backup card under Settings therefore shows the real schedule, the next run, and from when a missing run counts as overdue. If a systemd timer runs the backup instead of cron, it reports its own next elapse directly.',
    'ops_restore_heading' => 'Downloading or restoring a backup',
    'ops_restore_p1' => 'Both happen on the Docker host, not in the portal. A usable backup under Docker/backups/ consists of a database dump, configuration archive and matching SHA-256 manifest with the same timestamp. The newest files of each artifact type are retained (14 by default); older files are deleted separately for each type.',
    'ops_restore_p2' => 'The portal deliberately offers neither a download nor a restore. Only the status directory is mounted read-only into the PHP container, never the backups themselves, because the configuration archive contains the .env with the database root password. The restore steps are in the project directory on the server (not reachable inside the portal): docs/operations/backup.md. To check whether a dump is usable, scripts/restore_test.sh loads it into a throwaway container without touching the running stack.',
    'ops_restore_p3' => 'The JSON export of a mission is a transfer path between environments; the CSV export of a list is only a table export for reports. Neither is a backup: the files carry neither users nor credentials, MAC addresses or MECM ids, and cannot restore an environment.',
    'ops_backup_heading' => 'Backup symptoms and actions',
    'ops_backup_failed' => 'State "Failed": check the cron log and the error message on the card; is the stack running, is there free space?',
    'ops_backup_stale' => 'State "Stale": the expected run did not happen, grace window included; check the cron entry and test the script manually once.',
    'ops_backup_disk_low' => 'State "Low disk space": the backup volume is filling up; check retention or disk space.',
    'ops_backup_unknown' => 'State "Unknown" after an update: the status mount is missing or the script never ran; check the mount and start a backup.',
    'ops_backup_more' => 'Details and the full troubleshooting table are in the project directory on the server (not reachable inside the portal): docs/operations/backup.md.',
];
